# Git decides line endings per clone, from whatever core.autocrlf the developer happens to have set.
# That is a guess, and for three kinds of file in this repository it is a guess that breaks something
# quietly. This file makes the answer the same in every clone.
#
# Keep it saying what .editorconfig says: the two are read by different tools for the same reason, and a
# rule stated in both places must state the same thing.

* text=auto

# The authoring surface. CRLF in the working tree, LF in the repository - the split CONTRIBUTING.md
# describes.
*.cs         text eol=crlf
*.csproj     text eol=crlf
*.props      text eol=crlf
*.targets    text eol=crlf
*.slnx       text eol=crlf
*.md         text eol=crlf
llms.txt     text eol=crlf
llms-full.txt text eol=crlf
*.json       text eol=crlf
*.editorconfig text eol=crlf

# Read by Postfix and Docker inside the Linux container, where a stray CR is taken as part of the value
# rather than as encoding: "milter_protocol = 6" would be read as the version "6\r".
*.cf         text eol=lf
Dockerfile*  text eol=lf

# Run by a POSIX shell. A CR after the shebang turns the interpreter into a command that does not exist,
# and the CI runner is Linux.
.githooks/** text eol=lf
*.sh         text eol=lf
*.yml        text eol=lf

# A byte-exact fixture: MilterTests compares the delivered body against these bytes, so the line endings
# are part of the test rather than a formatting choice. Pinned rather than left to text=auto so that the
# bytes are the same on every machine.
MultiChunkBody.txt text eol=crlf

*.png binary
